BE-300 2.4.18: emergency printk instrumentation of the mount path.

THROW-AWAY DIAGNOSTIC PATCH.  Sprinkles printk(KERN_EMERG ...) markers
at every potentially-blocking call from sys_mount down through
proc_read_super so the last marker on the console identifies which
call swallowed control flow when `mount -t proc proc /proc` hangs.
Marker prefix is "BE300DBG:" so it greps cleanly out of the boot log.

Drop (or rename to 0007-DISABLED-mount-debug-printk.patch) once the
mount stall is fixed and the precise-fix patch lands.

console_loglevel is forced to 15 in proc_root_init() so KERN_EMERG
(level 0) reaches the BE-300 SIU UART poll-write console path
regardless of cmdline `loglevel=` settings.  That UART poll path is
the only proven-working visibility channel on this build (the BE-300
serial driver's tty layer is broken for userspace write/close, and
fbcon renders silently — see the build_be300_2_4_kernel.sh rcS notes).

The instrumented call chain matches the proc-mount path:

  sys_mount -> do_mount -> do_add_mount -> do_kern_mount
                                              -> get_sb_single (FS_SINGLE)
                                                   -> alloc_super
                                                   -> grab_super
                                                        -> down_write(s_umount)
                                                   -> do_remount_sb
                                                        -> shrink_dcache_sb
                                                        -> fsync_super
                                                        -> s_op->remount_fs
                                                   -> read_super (first-time)
                                                        = proc_read_super
                                                          -> read_lock(tasklist_lock)
                                                          -> d_alloc_root

